WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  lost for decades beneath the center of  naples italy the bourbon tunnel is an  underground time capsule of the city's  history  this system of tunnels and caverns was  originally conceived in the 1800s as an  escape tunnel by king ferdinand ii  connecting the royal palace to the  military barracks but the king died  before its completion  the tunnel was then abandoned until the  early 1930s when it was used as a  storage facility for impounded and  contraband vehicles  many of which are still there today  [Music]  during world war ii the tunnel changed  uses again this time becoming a military  hospital and bomb shelter  after the war it turned into a dumping  ground for rubble destroyed cars and  pro-fascist marble statues  before it was sealed  and then forgotten  it was accidentally rediscovered in 2005  when government geologists were checking  the conditions of nearby quarries  now the galleria borbonica belongs to  the public who explore the areas before  them as they wander the ancient caverns  [Music]  150 feet underground  in 30 million year old caves  an unforgettable sight glitters in the  dark  the waitomo glowworm caves in new  zealand are home to about 30 000 endemic  arachnocampa luminosa glowworms  they spend their days spinning silk  nests from the ceilings  and then lowering threads to fish for  prey much like a spider  the luminescent bead like strings are  spellbinding  the glowworms use light to attract prey  and burn off waste  the glow is created by a combination of  chemicals given off by the glowworm and  the oxygen in the air  the caves are ideal for the worms as  they need a dark damp location where  their light can be seen  [Music]  the way tomo caves they call home are  named after the maori words wei which  means water and tomo which means hole or  shaft  and although they live deep underground  the journey to see them is never one to  disappoint  [Music]  in southern central bosnia just outside  the town of konyitz and behind a quiet  unassuming building in the mountains  lies a secret place  a place designed to survive a nuclear  apocalypse  welcome  to tito's bunker  [Music]  construction of the bunker was started  by joseph brostito the president of  yugoslavia at the height of the cold war  it took 26 years to complete and was a  well kept top secret for decades  the horseshoe shaped structure goes over  600 feet deep into the mountain and is a  maze of offices conference rooms doors  as well as living quarters for tito and  his wife  in the event of a nuclear strike the  bunker could protect 350 of the most  important political and military persons  in the country  the bunker was almost destroyed in the  early 90s during the bosnian war but  went untouched remaining a time capsule  from the cold war its bedrooms bathrooms  phones and typewriters still lying ready  for atomic war  today the bunker serves a more peaceful  purpose as a modern art museum housing  one of the most important and valuable  art collections in southern europe  built for war between east and west  tito's bunker is now a safe haven for  creative works from artists from around  the world  to wander this mausoleum in shiraz iran  is like stepping into the holiest of  light shows  [Music]  which roughly translates to king of the  light in persian  is a funerary monument and mosque a  beautiful holy place for thousands of  muslim worshippers  the structure is made of multicolored  pieces of tile and glass and is  considered to be the third most  important pilgrimage site in iran  initially started around 900 a.d as a  burial site for an important shia muslim  figure  the mosque continued to be expanded and  improved  the decision to adorn the mosque with  its iconic mirror ball decoration came  from queen tashkatun in the 14th century  she wanted the mosque to intensify any  light a thousand times over an homage to  the mosque's name  although meant to be a holy shrine an  important pilgrimage location for shia  muslims people of any faith can marvel  at the mosque's immense beauty  [Music]  there's been a secret under the streets  of liverpool for hundreds of years  [Music]  a mysterious network of tunnels built  200 years ago and stretching on for  miles was built by eccentric and  successful tobacco merchant joseph  williamson  there's evidence of tunnels built and  then immediately bricked back up again  and arches that lead to nowhere  but the purpose and the reason behind  building these  is unknown  there's theories of course  one that he and his wife were part of an  apocalyptic cult and these tunnels were  to be their hideout  another that its only aim was to provide  jobs for the men in the area to combat  the high levels of unemployment  a final theory is they were used for  smuggling or other secret operations by  williamson himself  construction on the tunnels stopped  immediately after williamson's death in  may 1840  and it wasn't rediscovered until 2001.  since then a team of local retirees has  made it their mission to excavate the  tunnels  but with no records to offer explanation  the purpose of the tunnels or even a map  of how far they span  has made it a mysterious and tough job  [Music]  in this curious lagoon the rivers may  run red  but rest assured it's water  not blood  although you could be forgiven for  confusing the two  [Music]  the red lagoon is located twelve  thousand feet above sea level in an area  of chile so remote that it wasn't even  known by the national tourism office  until 2009  and maybe that's a good thing because  much like the pyramids in egypt the  lagoon is believed to be cursed  legend says that the waters are owned by  the devil himself and he is responsible  for the mysterious disappearances that  have occurred along the shores  the thermal temperatures of the water at  100 to 120 degrees stop even wild  animals from approaching it  devils aside what causes this blood red  water well biologists studying the  lagoon recently traced it to algae  growing in the depths of the lake  still  much of this lagoon remains a mystery  a visit to this place is a reminder  there is still much to marvel at on this  planet  what you're seeing was built by one man  pebble by pebble  over 33 years  [Music]  one morning mailman ferdinand chaval  stumbled across an interesting looking  stone on his delivery route  he picked it up  and from there an obsession began  using stones he collected on his walk  cheval worked each night without any  formal training to build what is now  called the palais ideal  the final structure is 85 feet long and  300 feet high  it mingles chinese algerian and northern  european influences to create a  fantastic and completely original castle  of grottoes  [Music]  he opened the palace in 1870 for the  public to enjoy  in his own words he says  you start wondering if you have not been  carried away into a fantastic dream with  boundaries beyond the scope of  imagination  and this incredible bizarre and unique  place  does look like something you could only  see in a dream  [Music]  you

